# REPRO: a governed segment whose MEMBER BITS are keepout-culled while its
# SHIELDS survive, so counting shield rows as placed bits hides the stranding
# from the cull-risk escalation tier (`_escalate_dead_low_segments`, the R13
# fix in ndr_architecture.md §7.5).
#
# ── Why this vehicle is shaped the way it is ─────────────────────────────
#
# The tier only looks at NON-TOP seats and escalates them to a TOP layer, so
# the vehicle must deliver BOTH at once: a governed run seated LOW, and a TOP
# layer that can still host it.  Three constructions were tried and do not
# work — worth recording, because each fails for its own reason:
#
#   * `def_ndr ... layers M3,M4` seats the run LOW, but a mask holding no TOP
#     layer also removes the escalation TARGET — the tier falls back to the
#     highest ALLOWED layer, which is the one the segment already sits on, so
#     it refuses with "at its cell band's ceiling" and nothing moves.
#   * a `span_max` penalty on the TOP layer steers the planner off it, but it
#     re-shapes the chosen TOPOLOGY, and the keepout no longer meets the run.
#   * `base_cost_non_top 0` does not seat a trunk LOW either: the planner
#     prefers a TOP layer for a trunk whatever that penalty says.
#
# So the layer is FORCED, with an `edit_set_layer` + `edit_commit pin` the
# planner honours for the candidate directly.  M9 is declared LOW (it is the
# seat) and M5 TOP (it is the escalation target).  Pinning the layer is not a
# workaround here but the honest instrument: the vehicle is about what the
# TIER does with a given seat, not about which seat the planner picks.
#
# ── The cull ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `shb` is `shield bus`: 4 bits flanked by 2 GND shields (demand 6 slots).
# The blocks are deliberately THIN, so the trunk's perpendicular interval is
# ~24 units — about 12 signal tracks — and the run cannot simply slide away
# from the keepout the way it does on a tall face.
#
# The keepout takes out the two CENTRE tracks over PART of the span:
#
#   * centre, so neither side leaves 6 contiguous clear tracks and the
#     span-clear-first placer is forced to straddle it;
#   * only two tracks, so the run's OUTERMOST rows — the shields — sit
#     outside the hole and survive the cull that removes the member bits;
#   * partial span, clear of the midpoint (550), so every track stays
#     midpoint-clear and the fallback pool is what the placer lands on.
#
# The result: 2 member bits placed, 2 shields placed, 4 member bits owed — so
# counting shield rows reads 4 of 4 ("nothing stranded, skip") while the truth
# is 2 of 4.  That is the exact arithmetic the R13 fix corrects.
#
# A/B with the `is_shield` exclusion removed: 4 net segments placed, 2 bits
# unplaced, 2 violations, and no CULL-HEAL line at all.  With it: 6 placed, 0
# unplaced, clean, `CULL-HEAL … opens 2->0`.

add_block a 0   94 200  118
add_block b 900 94 1100 118

add_bus sh_[4] a.p b.q

def_layer 4 M4 V 20
def_layer 5 M5 H TOP 20
def_layer 6 M6 V TOP 20
def_layer 9 M9 H 20

def_track_pattern 4 0 VDD 2 1 (_ 1 1)x12 GND 2 1
def_track_pattern 5 0 VDD 2 1 (_ 1 1)x12 GND 2 1
def_track_pattern 6 0 VDD 2 1 (_ 1 1)x12 GND 2 1
def_track_pattern 9 0 VDD 2 1 (_ 1 1)x12 GND 2 1

def_ndr shb shield bus net GND
set_ndr sh_ shb

# Two centre tracks, partial span, clear of the midpoint.
add_keepout 250 105 450 108 9

run_bundler STRICT
generate_topologies

# Force the trunk onto M9 (LOW).  Cost knobs could not do this: the planner
# prefers a TOP layer for a trunk whatever `base_cost_non_top` says, and a
# `span_max` penalty re-shapes the topology instead.  A pinned forced layer
# is honoured for the candidate directly, which is exactly the control the
# vehicle needs.
edit_topology 1 1
edit_set_layer 0 9
edit_commit pin
set_track_pitch 3
run_planner 1

run_nuts
run_detailed_nuts
check_design
report_wirelength
